WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.100
Welcome back to unintended consequences, episode 70, for July 26, 2026.

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:12.160
We're looking at a case today where the people involved were doing their best with the information they had.

00:00:12.820 --> 00:00:21.240
Reformers ended cash bail to free the poor before trial and found that removing one flawed tool requires designing what comes next.

00:00:21.940 --> 00:00:29.560
In January 2020, a man arrested in New York City on a low-level drug charge walked out of court without posting bail

00:00:29.560 --> 00:00:31.700
under the state's new reform law.

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:35.820
Weeks later, he was re-arrested on a more serious allegation.

00:00:36.380 --> 00:00:40.780
That case became one of several that entered political debate almost immediately.

00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:48.680
The reform had been written to keep people who had not been convicted from losing jobs and housing simply because they lacked cash.

00:00:49.580 --> 00:00:55.840
Instead, it surfaced the harder question of how any system decides who must wait for trial and who does not.

00:00:56.840 --> 00:01:02.100
For decades, observers across the political spectrum had documented the same basic problem.

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:10.600
Defendants too poor to post even modest bail remained in jail for weeks or months on misdemeanor or non-violent felony charges,

00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:12.800
while wealthier defendants went home.

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:22.520
Khalif Browder's three-year detention on Rikers Island for allegedly stealing a backpack became a widely cited example of the human cost.

00:01:22.520 --> 00:01:31.140
Advocates, defense attorneys, and some prosecutors argued that pretrial detention should turn on flight risk or danger to the community,

00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:34.080
not on the size of a defendant's bank account.

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:42.660
In the years after the 2008 financial crisis, when jail populations remained high despite falling crime rates in many cities,

00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:51.200
this equity argument gained traction among both liberal reformers and some fiscal conservatives concerned about the cost of local jails.

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:54.240
The underlying assumption was straightforward.

00:01:54.840 --> 00:02:02.500
If objective risk tools replaced cash, fewer low-risk people would be detained and overall public safety would not suffer.

00:02:03.260 --> 00:02:09.540
A defendant earning roughly minimum wage might need two weeks of take-home pay to cover a $500 bond.

00:02:10.300 --> 00:02:15.780
That arithmetic made release impossible for many even when judges viewed the charge as minor.

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:29.980
New Jersey moved first. After voters approved a constitutional amendment in 2014, the state implemented a risk assessment system in 2017 that sharply reduced the use of cash bail.

00:02:30.700 --> 00:02:37.920
Within two years, the pretrial jail population had dropped by roughly 40% according to state monitoring reports.

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:51.580
New York followed with legislation effective January 1, 2020, that eliminated cash bail for most misdemeanors and nonviolent felonies and required judges to use the least restrictive conditions.

00:02:52.220 --> 00:03:01.200
Early data from New Jersey showed high appearance rates and no immediate statewide crime surge, which supporters cited as evidence the approach could work.

00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:14.600
Skeptics, including some police unions and district attorneys, warned that the new rules removed judicial discretion in cases where a defendant's history suggested elevated risk even if the current charge was minor.

00:03:15.220 --> 00:03:22.460
In New Jersey, the risk instrument weighed factors such as prior convictions, employment stability, and residential ties.

00:03:23.120 --> 00:03:29.380
The state also funded additional pretrial services staff, though the expansion took time to staff fully.

00:03:29.380 --> 00:03:40.300
Once New York's law took effect, several high profile rearrests of release defendants received sustained media attention and prompted legislative pushback within months.

00:03:40.860 --> 00:03:53.740
The state responded by amending the statute in 2020 and again in 2022, restoring judicial discretion for more offenses and allowing prosecutors to argue for detention on additional grounds.

00:03:54.740 --> 00:04:06.780
Measuring the broader effect proved difficult. The reforms coincided with the onset of the COVID-19 pandemic, which altered policing, court operations, and crime patterns across the country.

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:18.500
Researchers at the Rand Corporation and elsewhere noted that isolating the impact of bail changes from these larger shocks required careful statistical controls that not every study applied.

00:04:18.500 --> 00:04:27.820
In New Jersey, some counties reported increases in certain offenses among release defendants, while statewide violent crime trends remained mixed.

00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:34.560
The causal mechanism most often discussed was the absence of a fully developed alternative to detention.

00:04:35.660 --> 00:04:46.940
Risk assessment instruments improved consistency but could not create new community supervision capacity overnight, and judges sometimes lacked intermediate options between release and jail.

00:04:46.940 --> 00:05:00.740
Third order effects included strained pretrial services budgets, renewed political pressure on prosecutors to seek detention in marginal cases, and public confusion over which charges still required bail.

00:05:01.540 --> 00:05:15.300
Supporters pointed to studies showing that overall rearrest rates did not rise dramatically in the first year, while critics highlighted specific repeat offense incidents and argued that aggregate statistics masked localized costs.

00:05:15.300 --> 00:05:24.080
New York's repeated statutory adjustments illustrate the most common response, incremental redesign rather than outright reversal.

00:05:24.820 --> 00:05:31.180
New Jersey has retained its core framework but continues to refine the risk tool and expand supervision programs.

00:05:31.960 --> 00:05:44.720
In both states, the conversation has shifted from cash bail versus no cash bail to questions of data infrastructure, funding for pretrial services and how to handle defendants with repeated low level arrests.

00:05:44.720 --> 00:05:55.800
Where new monitoring programs were funded, appearance rates stayed high, where they were not, judges reverted to detention more often, even under the revised statutes.

00:05:56.500 --> 00:06:05.840
Any policy that removes a visible lever must specify what will occupy the space it leaves, otherwise the system will improvise under pressure.

00:06:05.840 --> 00:06:15.560
Risk tools and release conditions are not self-executing, they require ongoing investment in supervision capacity and timely data feedback.

00:06:16.240 --> 00:06:23.660
When outcomes are measured only after political controversy erupts, adjustments become reactive rather than iterative.

00:06:24.460 --> 00:06:29.960
The bail reform experience therefore raises a standing question for other complex systems.

00:06:29.960 --> 00:06:38.220
What observable indicators will tell us early and reliably whether the replacement mechanism is performing as intended?

00:06:38.820 --> 00:06:54.320
That question applies equally to changes in housing regulation, environmental permitting or school discipline policy, where removing one imperfect control without a tested substitute often produces effects that appear only after the original lever is gone.

00:06:55.320 --> 00:07:06.980
Consider today's debates over algorithms that set prices for essential goods or platforms that moderate online content, in each case a familiar lever is being removed or restricted.

00:07:07.740 --> 00:07:17.640
What early specific signals would show whether the new arrangement is working before pressure forces another blunt correction, and that's where today's story leaves us.

00:07:17.640 --> 00:07:22.140
If it changed how you see one everyday decision, that's the whole point.

